1
00:00:00,143 --> 00:00:04,583
This 4K video keeps playing at its full
thirty frames a second while the AI

2
00:00:04,583 --> 00:00:08,905
scans it and an export writes a blurred
copy, all at once, in one C# app.

3
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:13,440
This last episode is the engineering
behind it: the system design, the

4
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:17,371
technologies, and the design patterns
that solve its real problems.

5
00:00:18,657 --> 00:00:23,170
There's one UI thread, and it never
decodes or blurs: it takes the input and

6
00:00:23,170 --> 00:00:25,248
puts each finished frame on screen.

7
00:00:25,676 --> 00:00:30,211
Playback, the scan and the export are
three pipelines with their own decoders

8
00:00:30,211 --> 00:00:33,981
and background tasks, and the
timeline previews run beside them.

9
00:00:35,124 --> 00:00:39,786
Producer–consumer with backpressure solves
a common problem: one part makes work

10
00:00:39,786 --> 00:00:41,943
faster than the next part can use it.

11
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:46,764
In our scan, the AI is often the slow
part, like the large model you just saw,

12
00:00:46,764 --> 00:00:49,133
and each 4K frame is
twenty-five megabytes.

13
00:00:49,657 --> 00:00:54,777
So a queue of four frames sits between
them, and when it's full, the decoder

14
00:00:54,777 --> 00:00:55,181
waits.

15
00:00:56,229 --> 00:01:00,273
That's what keeps memory bounded: each
pipeline holds at most nine, six or

16
00:01:00,273 --> 00:01:04,209
eleven frames, so a two-hour video
holds no more frames in memory than a

17
00:01:04,209 --> 00:01:05,029
ten-second one.

18
00:01:07,124 --> 00:01:11,670
Playback has seventeen milliseconds per
frame at sixty frames a second, and just

19
00:01:11,670 --> 00:01:13,943
decoding a 4K frame takes about fifteen.

20
00:01:14,371 --> 00:01:18,444
So it runs on a clock, and a frame that
will be late anyway is skipped before

21
00:01:18,444 --> 00:01:19,819
it's converted or blurred.

22
00:01:20,248 --> 00:01:25,308
Everything that moves changes only pixels,
a transform or a retained drawing, so

23
00:01:25,308 --> 00:01:27,143
WPF never runs a layout pass.

24
00:01:27,571 --> 00:01:32,333
Measured, 4K60 plays at sixty frames
a second, with zero layout passes.

25
00:01:33,476 --> 00:01:37,921
A one-slot mailbox solves the problem of
requests arriving faster than you can

26
00:01:37,921 --> 00:01:40,486
serve them, when only
the newest one matters.

27
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:45,068
Dragging the playhead fires a burst of
seeks, and if each one were served in

28
00:01:45,068 --> 00:01:47,524
turn, the picture would
fall behind the mouse.

29
00:01:48,048 --> 00:01:52,696
So a seek only writes its target into
one slot, each new one replaces the last,

30
00:01:52,696 --> 00:01:55,286
and the decoder goes
straight to the newest.

31
00:01:57,267 --> 00:02:01,596
Hovering over the timeline should show
a thumbnail at once, but on a 4K clip,

32
00:02:01,596 --> 00:02:04,238
one seek and one read
take about half a second.

33
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:08,699
So a background job with its own decoder
builds up to two hundred and forty

34
00:02:08,699 --> 00:02:12,947
thumbnails ahead of time, and on hover,
the UI thread just looks up the nearest

35
00:02:12,947 --> 00:02:14,076
one, in microseconds.

36
00:02:15,219 --> 00:02:19,196
Debouncing solves the problem of events
arriving far faster than you can act on

37
00:02:19,196 --> 00:02:19,448
them.

38
00:02:19,971 --> 00:02:24,415
Moving the mouse asks for a new frame
many times a second, and each exact frame

39
00:02:24,415 --> 00:02:25,990
takes half a second to read.

40
00:02:26,514 --> 00:02:30,680
So the app waits until the pointer
rests for a hundred milliseconds, then a

41
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:34,902
second reader fetches the exact frame,
and a newer request cancels the older

42
00:02:34,902 --> 00:02:35,124
one.

43
00:02:36,171 --> 00:02:40,446
And the job publishes each new thumbnail
as a fresh copy of the storyboard, so

44
00:02:40,446 --> 00:02:42,419
the UI thread reads it with no lock.

45
00:02:44,495 --> 00:02:48,964
The scan looks at five frames a second,
skips the rest without converting their

46
00:02:48,964 --> 00:02:51,962
pixels, and on the RTX, the
AI waits for the decoder.

47
00:02:53,105 --> 00:02:57,378
The Strategy pattern solves the problem
of several ways to do one job, chosen

48
00:02:57,378 --> 00:02:59,543
without changing the code that uses it.

49
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:05,366
Our scan only knows IObjectDetector,
but each model family answers in its own

50
00:03:05,366 --> 00:03:10,733
format: YOLO26's boxes, read the old way,
would have been junk, with no error.

51
00:03:11,257 --> 00:03:15,629
So each family gets a profile that checks
and reads its output, and the scan

52
00:03:15,629 --> 00:03:19,943
never changes. The blur, pixelate and
black-box effects are strategies too.

53
00:03:21,324 --> 00:03:25,520
A decorator solves the problem of
adding a step to some objects, without

54
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:27,152
touching them or the others.

55
00:03:27,676 --> 00:03:32,338
Classic models report several boxes per
face and need non-maximum suppression,

56
00:03:32,338 --> 00:03:36,760
but YOLO26 removes its own, and a second
pass could drop one of two people

57
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:37,238
hugging.

58
00:03:37,762 --> 00:03:42,067
So NMS is a decorator, wrapped around
just the decoders that need it.

59
00:03:43,114 --> 00:03:47,670
If you choose two models, a composite
runs both on every frame, and still looks

60
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:49,400
like one detector to the scan.

61
00:03:49,924 --> 00:03:54,174
And the detection list hears about new
boxes through events, applied in batches

62
00:03:54,174 --> 00:03:57,886
four times a second, so thousands
of boxes don't flood the UI thread.

63
00:03:59,924 --> 00:04:04,514
The export can't skip anything, so three
stages run at once, with queues between

64
00:04:04,514 --> 00:04:08,875
them that wait, and with FFmpeg encoding,
it got about three times faster at

65
00:04:08,875 --> 00:04:09,219
1080p.

66
00:04:10,362 --> 00:04:14,983
A pure function solves the problem of
two places that must always agree: same

67
00:04:14,983 --> 00:04:17,143
input, same answer, no hidden state.

68
00:04:17,667 --> 00:04:22,416
Our preview blur and our export must
blur exactly the same faces, or what you

69
00:04:22,416 --> 00:04:24,143
reviewed isn't what you get.

70
00:04:24,667 --> 00:04:29,118
So both call one pure function,
RedactionPlanner.Plan, which decides from the

71
00:04:29,118 --> 00:04:32,933
boxes, the regions and the settings
alone, so they can't disagree.

72
00:04:34,314 --> 00:04:38,869
An immutable snapshot solves the problem
of reading data that someone else keeps

73
00:04:38,869 --> 00:04:39,381
changing.

74
00:04:39,905 --> 00:04:43,658
The reviewer keeps working during an
export, and a file that's half the old

75
00:04:43,658 --> 00:04:45,810
review and half the
new one would be wrong.

76
00:04:46,333 --> 00:04:50,333
So pressing Export freezes a snapshot,
and the file matches that moment.

77
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,016
Undo in People works the same way: each
edit makes a new index, and undo just

78
00:04:55,016 --> 00:04:57,781
keeps the old one, with no
reverse code to get wrong.

79
00:04:59,857 --> 00:05:04,029
Ports and adapters solve the problem
of logic tied to one library, where

80
00:05:04,029 --> 00:05:06,752
changing the library
means changing everything.

81
00:05:07,276 --> 00:05:12,873
This app uses OpenCV, ONNX Runtime,
FFmpeg and WPF, and any of them could

82
00:05:12,873 --> 00:05:13,410
change.

83
00:05:13,933 --> 00:05:18,359
So the core declares the interfaces it
needs, small adapters implement them, and

84
00:05:18,359 --> 00:05:20,295
one composition root wires them in.

85
00:05:20,819 --> 00:05:25,881
That's how FFmpeg replaced OpenCV's
writer: one new adapter, one registration,

86
00:05:25,881 --> 00:05:31,009
one new method on the port, and a test
on every push keeps libraries out of the

87
00:05:31,009 --> 00:05:31,333
core.

88
00:05:32,714 --> 00:05:37,192
The Template Method writes the steps of
a job once, in a base class, and leaves

89
00:05:37,192 --> 00:05:39,800
one step open for each
kind of job to fill in.

90
00:05:40,324 --> 00:05:45,217
We have five background jobs, the scan,
the export, region tracking, people and

91
00:05:45,217 --> 00:05:49,924
the storyboard, and each needs start,
progress, cancel and failure handling.

92
00:05:50,448 --> 00:05:55,073
So ProcessingJob runs the same steps
for every job, start, the work, then

93
00:05:55,073 --> 00:05:59,889
completed, failed or cancelled, with a
state machine guarding each step, and

94
00:05:59,889 --> 00:06:03,057
each job only fills in
ExecuteAsync, its own work.

95
00:06:04,438 --> 00:06:08,366
A factory with a fallback solves the
problem of creating the best object a

96
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:10,914
machine supports, without
failing when it can't.

97
00:06:11,438 --> 00:06:16,733
On a real PC, the GPU might not load
a model, or FFmpeg might be missing.

98
00:06:17,257 --> 00:06:22,936
So a factory tries the best option first
and falls back, GPU to CPU, FFmpeg to

99
00:06:22,936 --> 00:06:26,286
OpenCV's writer, and the
work still gets done.

100
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:32,816
Today, only the face detection runs on
the GPU, while decoding, the blur and the

101
00:06:32,816 --> 00:06:34,381
encoding run on the CPU.

102
00:06:35,524 --> 00:06:39,066
I tried the blur on the laptop's
Intel GPU, and it was slower, fourteen

103
00:06:39,066 --> 00:06:43,008
milliseconds a frame against eight,
because every face box was copied there and

104
00:06:43,008 --> 00:06:43,257
back.

105
00:06:44,590 --> 00:06:49,095
So the next step is the whole pipeline
on the GPU: decode, scan, blur and

106
00:06:49,095 --> 00:06:53,352
encode, with no copies back and
forth and no extra color conversions.

107
00:06:53,781 --> 00:06:57,286
That should be much faster, but I
haven't built or measured it yet.

108
00:06:58,143 --> 00:07:02,192
Here's the whole design on one map, with
each pattern placed where it solved a

109
00:07:02,192 --> 00:07:02,867
real problem.

110
00:07:03,438 --> 00:07:05,152
Thanks for following the series.

